{"name":"Bodega Bay","hex":"#5e81c1","fetched":"2026-10-11","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"Bodega Bay","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Proper noun","definitions":["A census-designated place in Sonoma County, California, United States."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/Bodega_Bay"}],"entries":[{"title":"Bodega Bay","blocks":[{"text":"Bodega Bay is a shallow, rocky inlet of the Pacific Ocean on the coast of northern California in the United States. It is approximately 5 mi (8 km) across and is located approximately 40 mi (60 km) northwest of San Francisco and 20 mi (32 km) west of Santa Rosa. The bay straddles the boundary between Sonoma County to the north and Marin County to the south. The bay is a marine habitat used for navigation, recreation, and commercial and sport fishing."}],"image":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/a/a5/Wpdms_usgs_photo_bodega_bay.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bodega_Bay","wikidata":"Q2088977"},{"title":"Bodega","blocks":[{"text":"A bodega is a small owner-operated convenience store serving hot and prepared food, often open late hours and typically with ethnic market influences. The New York City Department of Health defines a bodega as any store of sufficient size \"that sells milk, meat, or eggs but is not a specialty store and doesn't have more than two cash registers\". Most famously located on New York City's street corners and associated with immigrant communities as well as the Puerto Rican community, they are romanticized for their convivial culture and colorful character. As of 2020, there were an estimated 13,000 bodegas across New York City."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/06/Corner_Bodega_%2848213980756%29.jpg/330px-Corner_Bodega_%2848213980756%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bodega","wikidata":"Q86753198"},{"title":"Bay","blocks":[{"text":"A bay is a recessed, coastal body of water that directly connects to a larger main body of water, such as an ocean, a lake, or another bay. A large bay is usually called a gulf, sea, sound, or bight. A cove is a small, circular bay with a narrow entrance. A fjord is an elongated bay formed by glacial action.\nThe term embayment is also used for related features, such as extinct bays or freshwater environments."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f1/Aerial_photograph_of_Langness_and_the_bay_at_Castletown_13_February_2014_-_geograph.org.uk_-_3847162.jpg/330px-Aerial_photograph_of_Langness_and_the_bay_at_Castletown_13_February_2014_-_geograph.org.uk_-_3847162.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bay","wikidata":"Q39594"},{"title":"Bay (horse)","blocks":[{"text":"Bay is a hair coat color of horses, characterized by a reddish-brown or brown body color with a black point coloration on the mane, tail, ear edges, and lower legs. Bay is one of the most common coat colors in many horse breeds."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/ca/BayMare.jpg/330px-BayMare.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bay_(horse)","wikidata":"Q900906"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/44/Juan_Francisco_de_la_Bodega_y_Quadra.jpg/500px-Juan_Francisco_de_la_Bodega_y_Quadra.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/44/Juan_Francisco_de_la_Bodega_y_Quadra.jpg/500px-Juan_Francisco_de_la_Bodega_y_Quadra.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Bodega Bay is named after Juan Francisco de la Bodega y Quadra, who explored the area in 1775.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AJuan_Francisco_de_la_Bodega_y_Quadra.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b1/Bodega_Bay_viewed_from_Dillon_Beach%2C_CA.jpg/500px-Bodega_Bay_viewed_from_Dillon_Beach%2C_CA.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b1/Bodega_Bay_viewed_from_Dillon_Beach%2C_CA.jpg/1280px-Bodega_Bay_viewed_from_Dillon_Beach%2C_CA.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b1/Bodega_Bay_viewed_from_Dillon_Beach%2C_CA.jpg/500px-Bodega_Bay_viewed_from_Dillon_Beach%2C_CA.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Bodega Bay viewed from Dillon Beach.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ABodega_Bay_viewed_from_Dillon_Beach%2C_CA.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e6/Pictures_1239.jpg/500px-Pictures_1239.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e6/Pictures_1239.jpg/500px-Pictures_1239.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Bodega Bay, photographed on July 16th, 2007.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3APictures_1239.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/ca/DirkvdM_baracoa_panorama.jpg/500px-DirkvdM_baracoa_panorama.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/ca/DirkvdM_baracoa_panorama.jpg/1280px-DirkvdM_baracoa_panorama.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/ca/DirkvdM_baracoa_panorama.jpg/500px-DirkvdM_baracoa_panorama.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Bay of Baracoa, Cuba","link":"https://